DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.16199258

This artifact contains the source code of ORBSLAM3, the EuRoC dataset (https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0278364915620033), along with OASIS modifications. OASIS itself is implemented through source code modifications that can be toggled on or off via YAML configuration files. In addition, this artifact contains modified build pipeline and production run pipeline to collect data to analyze and compare (via 10.5281/zenodo.16197736). Additional controller variants implementations include, also can be enabled or disabled through configuration files: Omega SLAM (https://doi.org/10.1145/3410463.3414636), PID SLAM (https://doi.org/10.5220/0007386508400848), along with Fixed Mask Size Variants.

In addition, general instructions are provided and links to cmake documentation is provided in case user would like to modify components. General instructions are provided to disable OASIS or have ORBSLAM3 run in original configuration. Note: A debug build is available as well, see 'build.sh' for enabling debug symbols and asserts.

# Repository Information
This copy was created on 7/20/2025, from https://github.com/allesrebel/ORB_SLAM3, commit 97984ac7637bc6af19ca5d01a466f1ab959314c9

# Directory Structure
Examples - contains examples utilizing ORBSLAM3 library for various sensor configurations and configuration YAMLs
Thirdparty - ORBSLAM3 dependencies (g2o, DBoW2, Sophus)
Vocabulary - A ready to use vocabulary provided by the authors of ORBSLAM3
evaluation - evaluation scripts to assess SLAM outputs
include - source code include directory
src - source code implementation directory
CMakeLists.txt - configuration and build instructions using CMAKE
Calibration_Tutorial.pdf - instructions to adapt ORBSLAM3 for your own sensor configuration
build.sh - build script for ORBSLAM3 library and example executables
run_all.sh - production run of all controller variant trials
run_example.sh - a quick test to ensure ORBSLAM3 is functioning correctly
run_mask_variations.sh - a production run across many different static masks

# Screencast example (monocular SLAM on a desktop screen capture)
Examples/Monocular/mono_screencast.cc            - live X11 screen-grab driver (needs DISPLAY)
Examples/Monocular/mono_screencast_images.cc     - offline driver: replays a directory of frames through SLAM
Examples/Monocular/mono_screencast_stream.cc     - cv::VideoCapture driver: any path/URL (file, RTSP, UDP/TCP MPEG-TS, etc.)
Examples/Monocular/gen_screencast_video.cc       - synthesises a parallax-rich screencast .mp4 with 3D-projected windows, a moving cursor, and window navigation (so monocular SLAM can actually initialise)
Examples/Monocular/ScreenCapture.{h,cpp}         - X11 capture wrapper used by mono_screencast
Examples/Monocular/ScreenCapture.yaml            - SLAM intrinsics & ORB params for the screencast example
Examples/Monocular/run_screencast_test.sh        - end-to-end image-driver test: ensures the video, extracts frames, runs SLAM, prints stats, dumps Map.ply

End-to-end run (headless): ./Examples/Monocular/run_screencast_test.sh
Outputs: test_data/run/{run.log, KeyFrameTrajectory.txt, Map.ply, SessionInfo.txt, TrackingTimeStats.txt, LBA_Stats.txt, LocalMapTimeStats.txt, ExecMean.txt}
Visualise the map: meshlab test_data/run/Map.ply
See README.md section 10 for full details, environment overrides (SCREENCAST_VIDEO_URL, SCREENCAST_SECONDS, …) and the live X11 invocation.

# Screencast regression test suite
tests/screencast/run_all_tests.sh         - top-level runner: invokes every sub-test and prints a final pass/fail summary
tests/screencast/test_video_file.sh       - Test 1: decodes screencast_test.mp4 via cv::VideoCapture (mono_screencast_stream)
tests/screencast/test_video_stream.sh     - Test 2: streams the video as TCP MPEG-TS over loopback and runs SLAM against the stream
tests/screencast/test_x11_session.sh      - Test 3: spins up Xvfb, plays the video with mpv, jiggles mouse, runs mono_screencast
tests/screencast/validate_trajectory.sh   - shared TUM-trajectory sanity validator (KFs, path length, quaternion norms, monotonic timestamps)
tests/screencast/common.sh                - shared helpers (paths, ensure_test_video, parse_kfs/mps)

Run everything:                ./tests/screencast/run_all_tests.sh
Run a subset:                  ./tests/screencast/run_all_tests.sh file stream
Run a single test directly:    ./tests/screencast/test_video_file.sh   (or test_video_stream.sh / test_x11_session.sh)
Validate any trajectory:       ./tests/screencast/validate_trajectory.sh <traj.txt> <min_kfs> <min_path>

Test 3 prerequisites: apt-get install -y xvfb xdotool x11-utils mpv
Per-test outputs: test_data/test_video_file/, test_data/test_video_stream/, test_data/test_x11_session/ (each with KeyFrameTrajectory.txt + Map.ply + run.log)

# Using DockerHub Base Image on Non-Native Architectures
A container can be used to process the data, if docker is supported on your architecture:
https://hub.docker.com/layers/library/ubuntu/jammy-20250714/images/sha256-da5fdf346e5313bef2a3dd2476c0251d48103213a5e3a0cb3afbb8909f3cf50f

An example using ubuntu:jammy (above):
"sudo docker pull ubuntu:jammy"
"docker run -it ubuntu:jammy"
"cd $HOME"
<Download+extract Artifacts> 

For convenience a container built using the above instructions is provided on docker hub at: https://hub.docker.com/repository/docker/allesrebel/oasis-orbslam3/general - meant for non-nvidia systems. See below for instructions on Nvidia devkits.

# Manual Reproduction Steps (Ubuntu)
In the Docker Base Image, with the artifacts extracted:
"apt update"
"apt install -y cmake python3 python3-pip binutils wget g++"
"./build.sh"

Will launch cmake and download the EuRoC MAV dataset, along with any ORBSLAM3 dependencies. 

# DockerHub Preconfigured Image Instructions
! This section is applicable to Non-Nvidia Devices
1. Pull the docker image from dockerhub: 
docker pull allesrebel/oasis-orbslam3:latest

2. Run the container
docker run -it allesrebel/oasis-orbslam3

3. Execute 'run.sh' to perform a production run
./run_all.sh

4. Examine results in home folder

# Nvidia Jetson DockerHub Container information
! This section is only applicable if using an Nvidia Jetson DevKits
1. Install and configure https://github.com/dusty-nv/jetson-containers
	- This updates the docker runtime to use nvidia container version
2. Run the following script: "$($JETSON_CONTAINERS/autotag opencv l4t-ml)"
	- Where $JETSON_CONTAINERS is the install location of jetson-containers above
3. "apt update", "apt install -y cmake wget", "./build.sh" to download dataset, and build ORBSLAM3 with OASIS
	- Then experiments as normal can be performed ('run_all.sh', 'run_example.sh', etc)

# Original data collection information
This artifact contains trials performed on an Intel Platform with the following specifications:
10-core Intel Core i7-6950X CPU
64 GB 256-bit DDR4 RAM

As well as on a Nvidia Jetson AGX Orin:
8-core Arm Cortex-A78AE CPU
16 GB 128-bit LPDDR5 RAM

With dataset pulled directly from the EuRoC MAV dataset: https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0278364915620033
Note: Alternatively you can pull from: https://projects.asl.ethz.ch/datasets/doku.php?id=kmavvisualinertialdatasets

# ORBSLAM3 Configuration
ORBSLAM3 itself is built as a loadable library. There are several different Examples utilizing this library in the Examples Directory.
Provided is also controller configuration for each executable through YAML files. Note, the YAML files also contain default SLAM settings which can be modified if desired. For our work, we kept the defaults provided by the original authors of ORBSLAM3. 
For example to disable OASIS, a comparison between "Examples/Stereo-Inertial/EuRoC.yaml" and "Examples/Stereo-Inertial/EuRoC_oasis.yaml" would reveal that "System.enableOasis: 1" is present for the OASIS configuration. Removal of this in the configuration file would disable OASIS.
To support tracking of source code, the example executable also print out the commit hash of the git repo along with configuration read from the YAML file. For example, during execution with OASIS enabled, stdout would print "OASIS enabled" along with regular SLAM configurations.  

# Experimental Configuration
By default, when doing a production run 'run_all.sh', each controller is done across all environments in the EuRoC MAV dataset. These trials are done multiple times and each trial is done randomly to reduce impact from confounders. These can be directly modified as desired, for example, if more trials are required per controller variant, 'run_all.sh':51 can be modified. Or if specific variants need to be removed, lines 57-65 list YAML configurations and the resulting output name. Of if specific environments need to be removed or added, modification of line 54 and removing  the specific part of the dataset can be done. Each of these modifications has a direct impact on production run duration.

# Configuration Variants
"normal" refers to the configuration ORBSLAM3 authors provided
"deadlines" refers to configuration processing time for each frame is accounted for, and compared against next frame's timestamp. If exceeded, the next frame is skipped. Multiple frames may be skipped to account for excessive processing times
"oasis" refers to the dynamic masking based on computational budget controller (our method), deadlines are considered
"fov" refers to utilizing a static mask size
"pid_slam" refers to an implementation of https://doi.org/10.5220/0007386508400848
"omega_slam" refers to an implementation of https://doi.org/10.1145/3410463.3414636

"deadlines" can generally be applied to any method that doesn't normally consider deadlines. For example, performing an experiment with a static mask, while considering deadlines would be the "fov_deadlines" variant.

# Output Data
Each trial output may contain the following files:
	ExecMean.txt (all)
	LBA_Stats.txt (all)
	LocalMapTimeStats.txt (all)
	SessionInfo.txt (all)
	TrackingTimeStats.txt (all)
	cellManager.txt (oasis)
	f_dataset-<EuRoC dataset>_<Sensor configuration>_<variant name>.txt (all)
	kf_dataset-<EuRoC dataset>_<Sensor configuration>_<variant name>.txt (all)

The common files labeled as (all) are found in all trials. While "cellManager.txt" is a readout of the oasis mask changes and is only generated for the OASIS variant trials. Of note are the last two files, "f_dataset" and "kf_dataset" which are the camera trajectory and key frame trajectory respectively.

